00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:06.906
LeJEPA: provable and scalable self-supervised learning without
the heuristics.

00:00:07.356 --> 00:00:14.172
By Randall Balestriero and Yann LeCun, from Brown, Meta-FAIR
and NYU.

00:00:15.522 --> 00:00:29.898
A Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture takes two views of
the same thing — two crops, two augmentations, two video

00:00:30.348 --> 00:00:35.604
That objective captures what the views share, which is the
semantic content you wanted.

00:00:36.054 --> 00:00:43.614
But it has a trivial solution. Map every input to the same
vector and the prediction is perfect.

00:00:44.064 --> 00:00:52.632
So every JEPA needs a second ingredient to stop that. The
design of that second ingredient is what this paper is about.

00:00:53.982 --> 00:00:59.862
The failure is not one thing. Complete collapse maps
everything to a single point.

00:01:00.312 --> 00:01:10.416
Dimensional collapse is subtler: the embeddings spread out,
but only within a low-dimensional subspace, so most of the

00:01:10.866 --> 00:01:19.026
And even without collapse, the embeddings can be anisotropic —
stretched far more along some directions than others.

00:01:19.476 --> 00:01:29.484
The paper's first claim is that all three are the same
problem: the embedding distribution has the wrong shape, and

00:01:30.834 --> 00:01:51.426
Current recipes prevent collapse with a stack of tricks. Stop-
gradient. A teacher-student pair with a carefully tuned

00:01:51.876 --> 00:01:58.524
The authors call this a game of whack-a-mole, and the
criticism is precise rather than rhetorical.

00:01:58.974 --> 00:02:12.870
